{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head b755cbcc778652362a052d6fcc3c48a78ce13345 on builder/o2-on-landed-lv5-20261121, all 20 artifacts, for request 5368da60 under my promise 8186d20b. The implementation is O2 (request a9455de4, promise 51aed59d). The reporting artifact is the O2 note.\n\nThe head is 4 commits on origin main cfcfb7c9. `git merge-tree --write-tree origin/main b755cbcc` gives tree 57978038, the head's own tree, so the head is a fast-forward of main. `check-commit-messages cfcfb7c9..b755cbcc` checks 4 commits and flags none. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, `git diff --check` is clean, and no added line names a local path.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **The gate.** END e52d3fc0 passed on source abbc80da (tree 10b114ba, 0 changed files, exit 0, 1,401.57 s). The review head differs from abbc80da in the note alone. `gate.txt` hashes to `341366b8…`.\n- **The note's hashes.** All 9 hashes in the note's tables match the files under `2026-11-21-o2-on-lv5-gate/`: the four focused logs, the Effects check, the statechart log, report and diagram, and the gate.\n- **Scope.** The 20 artifacts filed at this head equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only cfcfb7c9 b755cbcc`.\n- **The kernel status correction.** `docs/kernel.md` changes one sentence in section 7, from \"The level is designed and not built\" to \"The first version of the level is built.\" That is true after LV5 landed and the Workers deployed, and it is the point I noted on LV5. Planner direction 3fa68362 covers it. No promise changes.\n\n## The authored opening and the recognizer\n\n`authoredCutoff` is a scheduler hint and grants no authority. It returns a cutoff only for a narrow, fully checked form:\n- the entry is the accepted origin act that opens an item and sends exactly one unconditional `tell` at ordinal 0;\n- the tell's target is a fixed, required scope slot filled directly from an intent field, and the sent body equals the declared message built from the intent's fields;\n- exactly one timed rule applies to the item's final state, and its deadline is a fixed, required time slot filled directly from an intent field;\n- the origin entry's own effects agree on the item ID, the destination, and the due time.\n\nAny condition, offset or ambiguity returns null, and the ordinary dispatch schedule stays. The recognizer re-derives the entry hash, the genesis and the scope from the stored bytes before it trusts anything. `outbox.ts` then requires the recognized target and message to equal the send's own row. The tests cover one accepted opening, changed sends and effects that never acquire a cutoff, and ambiguous or conditional forms that are refused.\n\n## The destination proof\n\nAfter a real retry, the dispatcher asks the namespace for `nativeLiveTarget`:\n- the scope index answers only for a scope it already knows, and it does not initialize or register an unknown target, in register mode too (`scope-index.ts`);\n- the target object reads its stored genesis and canonical pinned definition in one bounded capture, with no turn and no alarm recovery. It reports `live` or `other`;\n- the caller re-admits the birth against the full target reference (scope and genesis) and rejects a reply that claims a native definition is live;\n- one proof slot stays held until the underlying call settles, including after a timeout, so a slow target cannot stack proofs.\n\nThe level reply rests on the trusted native namespace, as the note and `docs/security/entry-points.md` state. A missing, malformed or late reply proves nothing and keeps ordinary dispatch. `nativeLiveTarget` is an internal namespace method with no HTTP route, and the security inventory lists it. The seals move for `object.ts`, `turn.ts`, `scope-index.ts` and `namespace.ts`, and `native-live-target.ts` is newly sealed. I read each source diff behind them.\n\n## The retry, the timed cutoff and the private receipt\n\n- **Receipt.** `turn.ts` stores the receipt in the same transaction that seals the timed entry, and only when the recognizer matches the pending origin, rule, item and due time. A rollback (for example `Full`) rolls the receipt back with it. `putLiveCutoffReceipt` inserts only for the exact request that is still dispatchable (not held, acknowledged, answered or diagnosed). SQL triggers delete the receipt on acknowledgment, result, diagnosis, hold or removal. The table is created lazily by an eligible sender. It sits outside the journal and replay.\n- **Dispatch.** The dispatcher re-reads the durable row (`#current`) after every asynchronous step and drops the work if the row moved. A proof of `true`, a prior `retry` answer and either a receipt or a passed deadline are all required before it gives up. If the deadline has passed and the timer has not fired, it drains the alarm once and re-reads the receipt. If the timer is still pending it re-arms in `drainRetrySeconds`. A committed receipt still gives up after a clock rollback. An attempt already in flight completes. A wake update that crosses the cutoff makes the dispatcher check again before it logs or sends.\n- **Diagnosis.** `judgeDiagnosis` is unchanged. Its finding follows from the attempt log alone. With a `retry` in the log it is `delivery-unavailable`, which runs no clause, keeps the request pending, and leaves a late authentic result eligible. Replay judges the same entry from the same log, so a diagnosis that the cutoff writes before the routing-refusal count is a valid entry. In `giveUp`, the judge refuses with `answer: false` unless the receipt and the current row still hold.\n- **Operations and the formal model.** `operations.ts` is byte-identical to the previous reviewed source (`b33908e9…`). The Effects seal for `sqlite.ts` is `a2dbe93d…`, which matches the file at this head. The Effects check passes with T19 and 7 distinct states, and the model keeps `AtMostOneSend`, `MarkBeforeEffect` and `NoDeadlock`. The note states that the model covers one attempt and does not cover the cutoff policy, and I agree.\n- **Witnesses.** `authored-cutoff-native.test.ts` receives a real retry from an active live scope. It checks the persisted receipt across eviction and clock rollback, the single timed diagnosis with no further sends, and the late authentic result on the existing path. Its negative controls are that an unanswered attempt authorizes nothing, a moved item with no timed receipt keeps ordinary dispatch, and a deadline reached while the retry is being armed fences the unsent attempt and keeps the original log. The native proof tests check that the capture leaves stored rows and the alarm unchanged, that an unindexed target is never activated, and that a timed-out read holds its slot. The baseline reproduction under `2026-11-21-lv2-o2-baseline/` shows the defect that the head removes: a live target retried through 63 s with no diagnosis.\n\n## Wake rate\n\nThe head adds no alarm class and no new schedule. It only moves an existing retry wake earlier, to no later than the authored deadline.\n- **Floor:** 0 added requests per hour when no send is cutoff-eligible.\n- **Per eligible request that is retrying:** at most one target read per attempt. Attempts run on the existing 1, 2, 4 … 300 s backoff, so a stuck request makes about 12 target reads per hour once it reaches the 300 s cap, and it stops at the authored deadline. Before this head, the same request retried without end past its intent.\n- **Worst case:** if the sender's own timed transition cannot commit after the deadline passes (for instance a full scope), the dispatcher re-arms every `drainRetrySeconds` (1 s). That is up to 3,600 target reads per hour for that request, at the existing drain-retry cadence. No other state makes it repeat at that rate.\n\n## Non-blocking\n\n- The note records the defect baseline and the negative controls, but no run that disables the clamp or the receipt requirement and shows a named test fail. The negative tests cover the same ground.\n- Add the deployment's DO-rate read to the planner's checklist for the hosted rooms, since senders are the only part whose request pattern changes.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The cutoff sits in the dispatcher under ruling 876695ba, uses the authored timed rule and the existing diagnosis, and adds no entry kind or reason code.\n\nSecurity: no finding. The recognizer fails closed. The proof reads only a known scope without activating it, readmits the birth against the full reference, and treats any bad reply as no proof. `delivery-unavailable` gives no clause and no authority. The receipt is private, tied to one exact request and released with it.\n\nSafety: no finding. An ambiguous form, an unproven or recorded target, a moved item, an unanswered attempt, a stale row or a lost reply each keep ordinary dispatch. A late authentic result remains recordable.\n\nLiveness: no finding. A proven live target stops retrying at its authored deadline. The proof read, the dispatch pass and the wake are bounded, and the timed transition retries at the existing drain cadence.\n\nSimplification: no required change.\n\nFact for the planner's deployment: the scope Durable Object code changes, so every room Worker that runs the scope object needs the deployment and a DO-rate read. The new SQLite table is additive and created lazily, and an older Worker ignores it, so rollback is safe. No DO class migration is involved.\n\nThis approval authorizes no push, landing or deployment. The planner decides the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b0df55ec44b760af70628dad104691eae446dfc8","binding":"assigned","head":"b755cbcc778652362a052d6fcc3c48a78ce13345","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8186d20bd10e0bccef8305c54ea0a67da23f90d5\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:51aed59ded8a27fd519421141cf0cc6cb4d51b7e\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8186d20bd10e0bccef8305c54ea0a67da23f90d5","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}